in order to prevent global warming from  reaching 1.5 degrees it's estimated that  the next 10 years are crucial for us to  collaborate across sectors to remove and  prevent carbon pollution which is also  known as greenhouse gases  an incredibly interdisciplinary open  data project called climate trace is  presently led by 10 entities and former  vice president al gore and is  collaborating with over 50 organizations  to come together and help detect where  carbon pollution is presently coming  from by country and even by industry  using satellite images and machine  learning  soon it will transparently publish where  pollution arises from a particular  factory a coal plant an agricultural  field an animal farm etc  the intention behind creating this  global inventory of emissions is to help  policy makers industry operators and  even consumers who may not only be  investing in high polluting  organizations and take actions quickly  to prevent a climate crisis  because the energy sector is currently a  top focus in this episode we walk  through how to generate a geospatial  classification model that passes the  image of a coal power plant from a  satellite called sentinel-2 and tells us  whether that plant is on or off  [Music]  thanks to data today we can understand  our natural world with far greater  richness than ever before by orders of  magnitude this now empowers us to build  amazing apps that can help us solve  complex social and environmental  challenges and so in this series let's  build an app together  meet gavin who is executive director at  whattime which is a non-profit that  helps the public understand how clean is  the electricity i'm using right now  using technology historically it's been  challenging to measure this  the global state-of-the-art mostly is  self-reporting just asking emitters how  much they emit and in some cases having  extremely expensive on-site equipment at  individual power plants and factories a  couple of governments around the world  but very few have open power plant  monitoring  and they monitor their heaviest sources  of emissions and provide that data free  to the public  for most sources of emissions around the  world there is no equivalent system and  so it wasn't the case that we could just  add up all of the open data and process  that into a global emission system we  actually needed to go out and create the  emissions databases that we were  aggregating to have a sense of global  emissions  climatetrace's web app and dataset uses  publicly available satellite data from  earth engine as well as privately owned  data  and all the compute processing and  classifying of the 59 trillion bytes of  data from over 11 000 sensors that come  from hundreds of satellites is done on  google cloud by using products such as  tensorflow and vertex ai for training  and labeling data bigquery and earth  engine for data analysis and kubernetes  engine for container management just to  name a few  without google this project really would  have never happened we submitted a grant  application for what we thought was a  little bit of a pie in the sky moon shot  we thought if this worked it would be  amazing google took a really big bet on  a project back when it was just an idea  they gave us a generous 1.7 million  dollar grant to make the project  possible  and then they doubled down they said you  know what we really want this project to  succeed and we're going to send seven  engineers full time for six months to  help accelerate this team of scientists  to make sure the machine learning and  the data engineering really are  successful  they then further doubled down to  support the coalition as this project  matured growing from an initial idea  around power plants to the new climate  trace concept of monitoring all ghg  emissions  i am beyond oppressed and very proud by  this project and send a warm-hearted  shout out to all the volunteers and  organizations who have made this idea  become reality  for those of us equally interested in  working with ai and satellite imagery  let's go a bit deeper on how we could  approach this problem  let's start with understanding our  inputs and outputs  our input data comes from sentinel-2  which is a satellite owned by the  european space agency  multi-spectral images of the entire  planet are taken every 10 days since  2015.  every pixel in these images  approximately covers a 10 meter by 10  meter area  when working with satellite data each  input image has dimensions of width  height and bands  bands are measurements from specific  satellite instruments for different  ranges of the electromagnetic spectrum  for example sentinel 2 contains 13  spectral bands if you are familiar with  image classification problems you can  think of the bands similar to images as  red green blue rgb channels  however when working with satellite data  we generally have more than just three  bands  as for outputs in this example the  output is a single number between zero  off and one on representing the  probability of that coal plant being on  next let's move on to building a data  set  climate climatrice went through the  labor of love of labeling lat long  locations of coal plants that correspond  to sentinel 2 images and shared it as a  csv each coal plant's location is on or  off at different times  next we use earth engine to get the band  values for a patch of pixels given their  lat long timestamp and on or off label  from each row of the csv file  we basically view the band values as the  data and the label as the answer  from this we randomly split this data  into training and validation data sets  and save them as tf records in cloud  storage  for this case we split it into 70  training and 30 validation data sets  this means the model studies and learns  from the training data set  while the validation data set is used  like an exam to make sure it's learning  to generalize to data it hasn't seen  before  next we use vertex ai google's machine  learning platform to train and validate  the model  we use a convolutional neural network  which is an ml technique that works well  with image data  it receives a patch of pixels where the  power plant is at its center  and as a friendly note each pixel in  sentinel 2 is approximately 10 meters so  we decided to use a padding of 16 pixels  around the plant  that means that our 33 by 33 patch is  around  330 meters by 330 meters  because of this our training data is a  consistent patch of 33 by 33 pixels and  the model returns the probability of the  plant being on  once we're happy with our model it is  stored into cloud storage as well  and in order to use that model to make  predictions of coal plants we deploy it  on cloud run and make it a predictions  service  and when done we celebrate with a dance  party  [Music]  so what's next if you would like to  review this process in greater detail in  the description below i have included  the link to an interactive notebook with  all the code used to build a sample  written by nikita a super passionate  peer of mine you can also find a blog  post and several helpful resources too  and community i'll leave you with a fun  fact from gavin about the project before  we end and if you found this content  helpful you can subscribe or leave a  comment on other ai for good use cases  you may have in mind cheers one fun  surprise about this project is i didn't  know how much the satellites that orbit  the earth today are mostly designed for  maps so so that we can all drive where  we need to go nearly every satellite has  been designed to land at about 11am  overhead  problem is what if you're trying to see  the emissions of something that doesn't  happen at 11am we've had to call  increasingly obscure agencies around the  world of various countries that happen  sometime to launch a satellite that's  good at seeing things at night  and we've gotten to know just how many  satellites are designed to 